From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: wait for the writing block, not for a deadline

0022 anchored a job's completion wait on the hardware's own account of the
kick, so how long a batched STREAM may be stopped being a property of the
driver's timing.  What it left is the other half: how long ONE program may
be.

Past the kick, the driver waits for the writing block's own completion
bounded by dpu_grace_us, a fitted 500 us.  That bound is on the last
program of the kick, and PC_DONE does not mean that program is finished
computing -- it means the program counter retired it.  On a job of one
task, where TASK_NUMBER = 1 and both PC_TASK_STATUS counters read zero
always, PC_DONE alone is the kick-over signal and it is raised while the
CNA/CORE/DPU pipeline still has the whole convolution to run.  So the
grace is asked to cover an entire program's execution, and every value is
too small for some program.

What that looks like from userspace is a hardware capacity bound, and it
wore one convincingly enough to be recorded as one.  A single 112x112 k7
ic32 convolution is bit-exact up to a plane height that FALLS as the
output-channel count rises -- 78 rows at two output-channel groups, 44 at
four, 32 at six -- with a full, correctly sized surface whose last rows of
the last output-channel group carry plausible wrong values.  No
"feature + weights <= pool" form states it: one group computes at a total
of 6888 CBUF granules where two groups fail at 5992.  What every one of
those walls has in common is WORK -- 766 to 830 MMACs, at a footprint that
falls 2.4x across them -- and work is a time.  Raising dpu_grace_us to 750
makes all of them bit-exact at unchanged shape, program and CBUF budget,
and restoring 500 brings them back [HW sweep, H96 MAX M9].

Two further measurements say the governing quantity is the program's
execution and not the bytes it writes.  At the same plane and a 3x3 kernel
-- 5.4x fewer MACs per output element, and a LARGER surface -- the same
convolution is bit-exact at every reachable height to 109, where the
output is 1.47 MB against the 516 KB the k7 wall sits at.  And at 64 input
channels, where the MACs per output element double and the surface halves,
the wall comes BACK.

So the deadline is the wrong shape for this wait, and there is no right
value for it.  The block raises its own completion; wait for that.  The
backstop 0022 added already bounds a job that never reports itself
finished, and it moves out of the kick-unfinished branch to cover this
wait too -- a job whose writing block never completes now retires there,
loudly, instead of silently handing back a truncated surface.

dpu_grace_us stays, as an optional CAP, and defaults to 0 -- wait for the
block.  Setting it non-zero restores the old bounded wait, which is what
makes the two comparable on one kernel.  Note that 0 used to mean "retire
on the tick after PC_DONE" and now means "wait"; nothing set it to 0.

The cost of this is paid by a job that raises no completion and does not
say so.  DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE and DRM_ROCKET_JOB_PPU_DONE are
advisory, and a job in the wrong class used to lose 500 us; it now waits
out the backstop instead.  That trade is deliberate in kind -- the old
failure was silent and produced a wrong surface, the new one is slow, is
logged with the core and the task count, and produces a right one -- but
it is not free, and how much it costs was measured rather than assumed.

Raising dpu_grace_us to 5000 on the old behaviour shows which submits ride
it to completion, because those are the ones whose wall scales with it.
Over a 58-gate list [HW sweep, H96 MAX M9] the whole run moves +1.0% and
every graph is inside noise -- MobileNetV1 623 ms either way, Inception V3
65917 -> 65351 ms -- so nothing on the library's own paths rides it: those
name their class (a matmul and a convolution as the wide-output class, a
pool as the PPU class).  Two probe rows do: 153 -> 535 ms and 214 -> 404
ms, which is ~85 and ~42 grace-bound submits, and both are raw test
submits that pass no class flag at all.

So the population that pays is jobs with no flag naming their writer, and
the fix for that is to name it, not to keep a deadline for everyone.
Measured with this patch applied, those two rows are 153 -> 10303 ms and
214 -> 4767 ms, which is the 125 ms backstop times the submit counts the
grace sweep predicted, to within 10%.  They stay rc=0: the surfaces are
right and the wait is long.  Every other row of the 58 is unmoved.

Measured with this patch applied, as an out-of-tree build of the same
source against the board's own headers [HW sweep, H96 MAX M9]:

  - the 112x112 k7 ic32 convolution at four output-channel groups is
    bit-exact at every height from 40 to 56, 51 runs, 0 wrong;
  - setting dpu_grace_us = 500 on this same kernel brings the failure
    back -- 13 of those same 17 heights are wrong, 30 runs of 51, with
    the wrong elements a suffix of the rows of the last output-channel
    group as before -- so the two behaviours are one A/B on one binary;
  - the unmodified series, rebuilt the same way, is 58 of 58 gates rc=0
    at taint 4096 in 205 s against the shipped module's 202 s, which is
    what says the rebuild is faithful before anything is read off it.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -40,10 +40,25 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(poll_interval_us,
 		 "RK3576 PC_DONE completion poll period, in microseconds (default 50)");
 
-static unsigned int rocket_dpu_grace_us = 500;
+/*
+ * An optional CAP on the wait for the writing block's completion, for a job
+ * that has one.  Zero -- the default -- waits for the block.
+ *
+ * It was a deadline of 500 us, and a deadline is the wrong shape for this
+ * wait.  How long a program's write DMA takes to drain is a property of the
+ * program: a single-task convolution whose drain outruns the cap has its fence
+ * signalled while the core is still writing, and the caller reads a full,
+ * correctly sized surface whose last rows of the last output-channel group are
+ * wrong.  Every value is too small for some program, so no value is right.
+ *
+ * The block says when it is done, so wait for it; ROCKET_POLL_BACKSTOP_US
+ * still bounds a job whose completion never arrives at all.  Set this non-zero
+ * to restore the old bounded wait.
+ */
+static unsigned int rocket_dpu_grace_us;
 module_param_named(dpu_grace_us, rocket_dpu_grace_us, uint, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(dpu_grace_us,
-		 "RK3576: how long to wait past PC_DONE for the DPU's own completion, in microseconds (default 500)");
+		 "RK3576: cap on the wait for the writing block's completion, in microseconds, or 0 to wait for it (default 0)");
 
 /*
  * The same deadline for a job userspace has told us raises no DPU completion
@@ -650,10 +665,12 @@
 	 * convolution or a matmul, the PPU for a pooling program, which enables
 	 * no DPU stage at all.
 	 *
-	 * That wait is bounded by the grace, so a task raising no completion at
-	 * all cannot stall behind one -- and because it now starts when the kick
-	 * is over rather than at the first poll, the grace is once again the
-	 * drain it was measured as.
+	 * How long that drain takes is a property of the program, so nothing
+	 * here puts a deadline on it: a job that names a writing block waits for
+	 * that block.  Only a job flagged as raising no completion at all takes
+	 * a timed wait, and that one is a blind SETTLE rather than a deadline.
+	 * The backstop below bounds both, so a job whose completion never
+	 * arrives still retires.
 	 */
 	raw = rocket_pc_readl(core, INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS);
 	if (rocket_poll_kick_over(core, raw)) {
@@ -661,13 +678,23 @@
 			goto complete;
 		if (!core->poll_kick_over_at)
 			core->poll_kick_over_at = ktime_get();
-		else if (ktime_us_delta(ktime_get(), core->poll_kick_over_at) >=
+		else if (core->poll_grace_us &&
+			 ktime_us_delta(ktime_get(), core->poll_kick_over_at) >=
 			 (s64)core->poll_grace_us)
 			goto complete;
-	} else if (ktime_us_delta(ktime_get(), core->poll_started_at) >=
-		   (s64)ROCKET_POLL_BACKSTOP_US) {
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The backstop is from the KICK and covers every way a job can fail to
+	 * report itself finished -- no PC_DONE, a task counter that never
+	 * clears, or a writing block that never raises its completion.  It has
+	 * to be tested on the same tick as the wait above rather than only when
+	 * the kick is unfinished, because that wait is no longer timed.
+	 */
+	if (ktime_us_delta(ktime_get(), core->poll_started_at) >=
+	    (s64)ROCKET_POLL_BACKSTOP_US) {
 		dev_err(core->dev,
-			"job of %u task(s) raised no PC_DONE in %u us; retiring it\n",
+			"job of %u task(s) did not report completion in %u us; retiring it\n",
 			core->poll_tasks, (unsigned int)ROCKET_POLL_BACKSTOP_US);
 		goto complete;
 	}
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
@@ -95,9 +95,16 @@
 	/* How many tasks this job's single kick covers. */
 	unsigned int poll_tasks;
 	/*
-	 * How long past PC_DONE to keep waiting for that completion, in
-	 * microseconds.  Latched per submit from the job's class (see
-	 * DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE) so the poll timer needs no job pointer.
+	 * How long past the kick to stop waiting for that completion anyway, in
+	 * microseconds, or ZERO to wait for it.  Latched per submit from the
+	 * job's class (see DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE) so the poll timer needs
+	 * no job pointer.
+	 *
+	 * A job that names a writing block gets zero: the block raises its own
+	 * completion and how long it takes to drain is a property of the
+	 * program, not of the driver.  A job flagged as raising none gets the
+	 * blind settle, which is a settle time and not a deadline.  Either way
+	 * the backstop still bounds the wait.
 	 */
 	unsigned int poll_grace_us;
 	/*
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
@@ -179,6 +179,13 @@
  *         object.  A caller that touches a few bytes of a large surface pays for
  *         those bytes; the whole-object forms are unchanged and a range_count of
  *         zero is exactly them.
+ * - 1.6 - a job that names a writing block waits for that block's completion
+ *         instead of for a deadline, so how long ONE program's write DMA may
+ *         take to drain stops being a property of the driver's timing.  No new
+ *         uAPI: the bump exists because on an older kernel a single program
+ *         whose drain outruns dpu_grace_us retires with its last output rows
+ *         unwritten -- silently, and looking exactly like a capacity bound --
+ *         and a caller that sizes its programs has no other way to tell.
  *
  * Userspace MUST NOT self-chain a job's regcmd layout unless the driver reports
  * >= 1.1: an older kernel does not know the flag, would run that chained layout
@@ -186,7 +193,7 @@
  */
 static const struct drm_driver rocket_drm_driver = {
 	.major			= 1,
-	.minor			= 5,
+	.minor			= 6,
 	.driver_features	= DRIVER_COMPUTE_ACCEL | DRIVER_GEM,
 	.open			= rocket_open,
 	.postclose		= rocket_postclose,
